From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:21:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] signal: the SEGV loop guard does not count a fault in FEX's
 code buffers

segv_handler's loop guard counts faults at one pc across every thread
and, at the fifth in a row, skips the faulting instruction and sets x0
to 0xDEAD0001. FEX raises every guest page fault it detects in a
translation (an instruction fetch from a page the guest keeps
inaccessible) from one pc, the deliberate read of address 0 in its
guest-signal raise, which it then rethrows to the guest. World of
Warcraft's x64 client keeps its code PAGE_NOACCESS and takes one such
fault per 4 KB page it runs; in run 20261101T181251 the fifth that
reached this handler, on the main thread after others on worker
threads, was skipped: the client's next read went through a register
holding 0xdead0c39 and the process ended (c0000005), no window.

A fault whose pc lies in one of FEX's live code buffers (the pool's
tail carves) is not counted and resets the count: FEX turns it into a
guest exception, and a guest looping on one fault is ended by the Mach
handler's per-thread fault-loop breaker (pp_fault_loop.h).

Linear: PLA-516, PLA-514
Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-11-01-wow-x64-fex.md
Offered-upstream: no; Madeira's iOS signal handler
---
 build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
index 51d4c67..028f08b 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
@@ -10992,7 +10992,22 @@ static void segv_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
         static uintptr_t last_fault_pc = 0;
         static int fault_repeat_count = 0;
         uintptr_t this_pc = PC_sig(context);
-        if (this_pc == last_fault_pc)
+        /* Playport (PLA-516): a fault in FEX's code buffers is a guest's fault, which FEX
+         * raises to the guest (a guest instruction fetch from a page it keeps inaccessible
+         * faults at one pc, FEX's guest-signal raise, on whichever thread and page): it is
+         * not counted here, where five in a row from any threads ended the fifth thread
+         * (World of Warcraft's x64 client, run 20261101T181251). A guest looping on one
+         * fault is ended by the Mach handler's per-thread fault-loop breaker. */
+        extern int ios_tail_carve_lookup_trylock( unsigned long long rx_addr, unsigned *idx_out,
+                                                  unsigned long long *base_out, unsigned long long *size_out,
+                                                  int *free_out );
+        int fex_code_free = 0;
+        if (ios_tail_carve_lookup_trylock( this_pc, NULL, NULL, NULL, &fex_code_free ) != 0 && !fex_code_free)
+        {
+            last_fault_pc = 0;
+            fault_repeat_count = 0;
+        }
+        else if (this_pc == last_fault_pc)
         {
             fault_repeat_count++;
             static unsigned long loop_storm;
